 "productionNotes": "The roadster total is one of the few R107 figures every source consulted here agrees on: 237,287 cars between 1971 and August 1989, stated by Mercedes-Benz's archive, Wikipedia, the mb107.com tables, the SLSHOP and Classic Trim Parts alike. The C107 SLC coupe adds 62,888 cars built between late 1971 and September 1981, giving a 107-series total of 300,175 - although the SLSHOP's fifty-facts piece prints 300,174, a one-car difference that reads as a transcription slip rather than a disagreement.\n\nThe mb107.com breakdown runs 350 SL 15,304; 280 SL 25,436; 450 SL 66,298; 380 SL 53,200; 500 SL 11,812; 300 SL 13,742; 420 SL 2,148; 560 SL 49,347. On the coupe side, 350 SLC 13,925; 280 SLC 10,666; 450 SLC 31,739; 380 SLC 3,789; and one line reading '500 SLC 1980-81, 2,769'. That entry is where the figures come apart. Wikipedia and Classic & Sports Car both split the five-litre coupes into 1,636 badged 450 SLC 5.0 and 1,133 badged 500 SLC, which sums to exactly 2,769. The mb107.com table appears to fold the earlier homologation cars into the 500 SLC line rather than to count differently, but no source consulted here says so, and both readings are recorded.\n\nClassic Trim Parts publishes a year-by-year roadster table reconciling to 237,287: three pre-production cars in 1970, a trough of 6,964 in 1974 after the oil crisis, a peak of 20,314 in 1986 - the R107's best year came in its sixteenth - and 7,866 in the final part-year of 1989. Roughly two thirds of production went to North America, an estimate of about 156,000 cars that several sources repeat and none breaks down by year. The SLSHOP dates the last car to 4 August 1989 and describes it as a 500 SL in Astral Silver.",
